**(i) Role of the HTML code (2 marks).** HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the markup language in which a web page is written. Its tags describe the *structure and content* of the page (title, headings, text, lists, links, images) and tell the web browser how to display each part of it.

**(ii) General name of the software (1 mark).** A **web browser** (for example Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge).

**(iii) Lines 8 to 12 (4 marks).** They create an **ordered (numbered) list**:

- `<ol>` (line 8) starts an ordered list and `</ol>` (line 12) ends it;
- `type = "I"` makes the numbering use capital Roman numerals (I, II, III...);
- `start = "3"` makes the numbering begin at 3 instead of 1, i.e. at III;
- each `<li>` (lines 9 to 11) is one list item.

The browser therefore displays:

```text
III. English Language
IV. Computer Science
V. Arithmetic
```

**(iv) Line 7 (5 marks).** The tag `<hr>` draws a **horizontal rule** (a horizontal line) across the page, below the line break of line 6 (which comes after the scrolling text). Its attributes are:

- `size = 16`: the line is 16 pixels thick;
- `width = "50%"`: the line is half as wide as the browser window;
- `align = "center"`: the line is placed in the centre of the page;
- `color = "blue"`: the line is blue.

(As printed in the question, the attribute is written `width "50%"` without the `=` sign. The intended meaning is the one explained above; strictly, a browser would ignore this faulty attribute and draw the line over the full width.)

**(v) Hyperlink to the page CSC595 (3 marks).**

```html
<a href="CSC595.html">CSC595</a>
```

- `<a ...> ... </a>` is the anchor tag that creates the link;
- `href="CSC595.html"` gives the address (file name) of the page to open;
- `CSC595` is the clickable text shown to the user. (If the page file has the extension `.htm`, `href="CSC595.htm"` is used instead.)
